"[{\"id\":\"1852113413\",\"title\":\"On a TC In, how can I add a Broker at a different \\\"From GMT\\\", while keeping the first Broker?\",\"text\":\"In order to add a Broker that starts at a separate \\\"From GMT\\\" from the first/original Broker, you must add the new broker twice. First insert new row with the new broker, the rate set to 0, and input the wanted From GMT in the To GMT: [image] Next insert another row, with the same broker, and the wanted rate, and now the wanted From GMT should populate: [image] You will now be able to have another Broker start at a preferred \\\"From GMT\\\".\"},{\"id\":\"1909358751\",\"title\":\"What is the firmed CP Qty field?\",\"text\":\"The firming qty field was added to capture information from pre-fixture negotiations, when a cargo's voyage is scheduled, the CP Qty will be recorded as the Firming Qty. The firmed CP Qty field is read-only. The value in the CP Qty field on a Cargo/VC-in is captured and stored when the voyage to which the Cargo/VC-in is attached is set to the status \\\"Scheduled.\\\" [image]\"},{\"id\":\"812351614\",\"title\":\"Why am I getting the message “Save error – unable to update voyages linked to cargo” when saving a cargo?\",\"text\":\"[image] You may encounter this error message when all the following conditions apply: The Voyage linked to this Cargo is in Completed status at the time of updating this Cargo, and There is at least one M-port in this voyage, but not every load/discharge line in the voyage itinerary is linked to an M-port. In order to be able to update and save the cargo, please ensure that there is an M-port for every L/D line in the itinerary of the voyage linked to this cargo.\"},{\"id\":\"1050116554\",\"title\":\"Why am I not allowed to adjust the Time Charter Bill date?\",\"text\":\"One of the reason why you are not able to adjust the Time Charter Bill date is due to the previous Time Charter Bill Date ending later than the current Time Charter Bill. i.e. The period to date a cannot be before the period from date. [image] To make an adjustment you have reverse the previous time charter bill transaction by doing the following: Search for the previous invoice ending with the date 30/11/19 in the invoice list \\\"View\\\" the transaction > Right click the payment and delete Reverse the transaction Select the TCO Bill > adjust the date to 29/11/19 Set status to Actual > Save Approve and Post\"},{\"id\":\"1196916991\",\"title\":\"Why am I receiving \\\"ERROR\\\"- The TCO Delivery Port should only be specified on the first voyage for TC Out?\",\"text\":\"[image] This error can occur if the voyage's TCO code is mistakenly entered in another voyage. You must check other voyages for the same TCO code, once identifying the error enter the correct TCO code. You will then no longer receive the above error for the current voyage.\"},{\"id\":\"1634828409\",\"title\":\"Why are Hire Adjustment items not included in the Hire Statement?\",\"text\":\"Hire Adjustment items in a Time Charter invoice (bill code HIADJ) are not included in the Hire Statement as they are typically used for unsettled items, for example an unsettled off hire that the counterparty has deducted from an invoice. As the operating party of the voyage, I have to process the invoice, with the intention of reversing it later once the off hire is settled. During the time whereby the off hire is still unsettled, I would not like this amount to be shown in the Hire Statement, so as to avoid giving the impression that the amount has been agreed on. Therefore, a Hire Adjustment would be used to represent the unsettled amount in scenarios like this.\"},{\"id\":\"679805231\",\"title\":\"Why are there two Payment Terms in a cargo contract?\",\"text\":\"Each Cargo contract has two separate Payment Terms . These two payment terms correspond to the Laytime Calculation and Freight Invoice of this cargo. Payment Terms in the Freight Invoice [image] Invoice % specifies what should be the default invoicing percentage for the given cargo when a new freight invoice is created. In the screenshot above, the Invoice % is 100. If we do not specify a number or enter 0 then IMOS will use the value of CFGFrtinvPercent for Invoice %. IMOS will apply the payment term DAID, highlighted in red, to the first freight invoice with the Balance % set to 0, highlighted in purple. Since there is no split, the Balance % is not editable. If we use a split, for example, 80% - 20%, then IMOS will use DAID payment term in the first freight invoice and DCD10 in the second freight invoice. Note : There are exceptions for Re-issue type of freight invoice and freight invoice where B/L Qty was not set in the voyage cargo. Also, for the above to work, the \\\"Bill By\\\" field needs to be set as \\\"Invoice Qty\\\".\"},{\"id\":\"3341189381\",\"title\":\"Why Are Tolls Categorized as Port Expenses in Deviation Estimates?\",\"text\":\"In Deviation Estimates , tolls are categorized as port expenses . This approach is a design choice made to simplify the Deviation Estimate P&L and streamline the calculation process. With this simplification it is easier to track and assess the overall impact of deviations in the Deviation Estimate P&L, while ensuring the accuracy and efficiency of the calculations. [image] This method is intentional and aims to enhance the ease of use for operators who rely on the Deviation Estimate to evaluate potential impacts on the Voyage Profit & Loss.\"},{\"id\":\"803078325\",\"title\":\"Why does my itinerary have duplicate passing points?\",\"text\":\"Home > Chartering FAQs > Why does my itinerary have duplicate passing points? Duplicate passing points may appear due to Fuel Zones updates in the Data Center. When new Fuel Zones are enabled, the old ones remain for historical reasons. As a result, you need to change what fuel zones are Inactive to ensure the most up-to-date port itinerary. Fuel Zones updates are regulated by deactivating older versions to avoid duplicate passing points in a voyage itinerary.\"},{\"id\":\"1857126476\",\"title\":\"Why doesn't the column view of a user defined field automatically update when amended in the Data Center?\",\"text\":\"Essentially changing the name of a UDF in the Data Center is not expected to update the respective column name in the list view. This is expected due to the design of the column names being saved as part of list views (to support things like custom names). To adjust the current column view it is expected to re-name the column to the field name entered within the Data Center. You may inactivate a UDF and then add a new one versus changing the name of an existing field to ensure the column name is correct. [image][image]\"},{\"id\":\"737149023\",\"title\":\"Why does the Laycan Range value of a Cargo/VC In as viewed from the Cargo/VC In List differ from the laycan window indicated in the contract itself?\",\"text\":\"The Laycan Range figure seen in the Cargo/VC In List is in GMT, whereas the Laycan From/Laycan To values indicated in a Cargo/VC In contract is in the time zone of the first load port. Therefore, it is possible for the laycan window of the Cargo/VC In to begin or end on a different date in the time zone of the first load port, as opposed to when converted to GMT. Take the below cargo as an example: [image] This would result in the Laycan Range column in the Cargo List displaying the below value: [image]\"},{\"id\":\"4000612402\",\"title\":\"Why do I have idle days in my TCO estimate?\",\"text\":\"The \\\"Idle\\\" column indicates the number of days a vessel remains in port without engaging in any port activity. Please note that, depending on how the itinerary and supporting data have been entered, idle days may appear at the port immediately before the TCO redelivery port. This is done to ensure that the total voyage duration&mdash;from TCO delivery to TCO redelivery&mdash;matches the planned TCO duration. If idle days are shown in the itinerary, it's because the specified TCO duration exceeds the total number of non-idle days on the itinerary. In such cases, the system inserts idle time to preserve the manually defined TCO length. If you prefer the TCO duration to be calculated automatically based on the itinerary alone (without idle day adjustments), you can enable the \\\"Trip TC\\\" option in the estimate properties. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"2065891353\",\"title\":\"Why don't I see any regions in the \\\"Regions\\\" section in Market Insights?\",\"text\":\"To be able to view/ retrieve regions, you should first create your own regions using the polygon tool. Regions are not pre-populated. Look at this article for more information on how to create regions and use the polygon tool.\"},{\"id\":\"3854139476\",\"title\":\"Why is bunker cost calculated in the TCTO Estimate?\",\"text\":\"In IMOS, if the TCTO estimates indicate bunker costs, it is essential to first verify the itinerary sequence of the estimate. The port itinerary sequence must align with the TCI Contract. Once the delivery port specified in the TCI Contract is designated as the first port within the itinerary sequence of the estimate, the bunker expenses will be automatically excluded from the TCTO estimate.\"},{\"id\":\"360775779\",\"title\":\"Why is my address commission not imported from my TC In into my Estimate?\",\"text\":\"If you have selected a Time Charter (TC) In for your Estimate and notice that the address commission rate specified in the TC In did not flow into the Estimate, make sure the voyage duration of your Estimate is greater than zero. Example When voyage duration is zero (i.e., the itinerary in the voyage estimate only has the commencing port), address commission is not yet imported from the TC In into the Estimate: [image] Once the itinerary of the Estimate is updated and the voyage duration becomes greater than zero, the address commission is updated in the Estimate according to the TC In: [image]\"},{\"id\":\"1634664620\",\"title\":\"Why is my TC broker commission not reflected in the Voyage Estimate P&L?\",\"text\":\"In order for TC broker commission to be reflected in the Voyage Estimate P&L, please first enable the configuration flag CFGTciBrokerCommOnEstimate . Once the flag is enabled, a new field Hire Comm (%) will be available in the voyage estimate, where you can indicate the appropriate broker commission percentage to be included in the Voyage Estimate P&L. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"5380112417\",\"title\":\"Why isn't the emission rebill showing on the Estimate column?\",\"text\":\"This is almost always caused by the configuration flag CFGDefaultTCOutCargoEmissionsSettlementType being set to N/A . When this flag is N/A, the system suppresses the rebill from the Estimate column, even if \\\"Include in PnL\\\" is checked on the contract. If this flag is changed to any non-N/A value ,the rebill will appear in both the Estimate column of the voyage PnL and the voyage estimate itself. [image][image]\"},{\"id\":\"4041113626\",\"title\":\"Why is the Bareboat Off-Hire amount not reflecting correctly in the voyage P&L?\",\"text\":\"The discrepancy is typically caused by a currency mismatch between: The currency of the Bareboat contract, and The currency used in the Miscellaneous Off-Hire entry When these currencies are not aligned, the system cannot calculate or display the Off-Hire amount accurately in the P&L, leading to missing or incorrect values. [image] To resolve the issue: Check the currency defined in the Bareboat contract . Verify the currency used in the Miscellaneous Off-Hire entry. Ensure both are using the same currency to allow proper calculation and display in the P&L screen.\"},{\"id\":\"871694557\",\"title\":\"Why is the Demurrage/Despatch missing in the Estimate PnL?\",\"text\":\"Demurrage or Despatch requires two fields to be populated before the system can calculate the amount and show it in the estimate PnL. The two things are the rates per day and the number of Demurrage/Despatch days. These two are entered inside the port itinerary details as shown below. [image] When entered correctly, the estimate PnL will show the Demurrage or Despatch row. [image]\"},{\"id\":\"3944251638\",\"title\":\"Why is the Estimate ID Not Auto-Generated When Creating a New Estimate?\",\"text\":\"The reason this issue happens is because the maximum limit of characters for the EstimateID has been reached for the specific user - which is why the system can no longer auto-generate them. The system's limit is 9 alphanumerical values, a combination of the user's initials, and the Estimate's number. Example: ADMIN-9999 Solution: To resolve the issue, the user's initials would need to be changed and shortened so that the system can auto-generate EstimateIDs from the beginning. Example: Change from ADMIN &rarr; ADM Once the initials have been changed, the user would have to log out and log into IMOS again for it to take effect.\"},{\"id\":\"3854794899\",\"title\":\"Why is there a difference between TCO Duration and Net Voyage Days in the Estimates?\",\"text\":\"The TCO Duration is derived from the TCI contract, while the Net Voyage Days are calculated based on the itinerary, which includes both Sea Days and Port Days. Additionally, it's important to note that the TCO Duration field is a text field, allowing users to enter the duration manually.\"},{\"id\":\"5228757005\",\"title\":\"Why is the TCI Bunker Adjustment not displayed in my Voyage Estimate ?\",\"text\":\"Three things must be true on the voyage estimate for this to calculate and display: The TC In contract code is linked to the estimate (via the Contract Code field) [image] \\\"Last TCI Voy\\\" is ticked in the estimate's Properties panel [image] The TCI contract has a Projected Redelivery quantity entered &mdash; without it there's nothing to calculate against. If all of those are already set up but TCI adjustment is not displayed, try unticking and re-ticking \\\"Last TCI Voy\\\" (saving and refreshing in between) to force the PnL recalculation or try removing the TC In Code from the estimate and re-add it. If the projected End ROB for a bunker grade is negative in the estimate, IMOS suppresses the adjustment line entirely rather than calculate against a quantity that can't physically exist (you can't have negative bunkers on board). There's nothing to revalue, so no line is shown.\"}]"